    "display_name": "Forecast the Weather",
    "long_instructions": "You’re building a program that predicts this weekend’s average temperature. To do this, this program has a `get_average_temperature` function. The function accepts two temperatures and returns the average.\r\n\r\n**Do This**\r\n\r\n1. Update the `get_average_temperature` function to return the average temperature. Remember that you can calculate the average of two numbers by adding them together and then dividing the result by 2.\r\n2. Call the `get_average_temperature` function to get the average temperature for the weekend. Save the result in a new variable.\r\n3. Use your new variable to print a message that tells the user the average temperature.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nFor functions that return a value, you can save the value in a variable when you call the function. For example, if a function called `get_temperature` returned a value, we could save it in a new variable called `degrees` with a line like this:\r\n\r\n```python\r\ndegrees = get_temperature()\r\n```\r\n\r\nPython follows the order of operations in math (multiplication and division happen before addition and subtraction), so if you want to control the order of operations, you can use parentheses. For example, this expression will be calculated as 14:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n2 + 3 * 4\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd this expression will be calculated as 20:\r\n\r\n```python\r\n(2 + 3) * 4\r\n```",
